---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { datasetStats } from "../lib/records";
import { disclaimer, site } from "../site";
---
<Base title="About" description="Sources, photo rules, and the limits of this unofficial Medal of Honor archive.">
  <div class="wrap">
  <p class="kicker">About this archive</p>
  <h1>About {site.name}</h1>
  <p class="lede">{disclaimer}</p>
  <p>
    The name of the site is set in one file, <code>src/site.ts</code>, so it can be changed
    without rewriting the pages. The archive does not use the Medal of Honor as a logo
    and does not use official seals.
  </p>

  <h2>What is in this version</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>{datasetStats.people.toLocaleString()} people</li>
    <li>{datasetStats.awards.toLocaleString()} awards</li>
    <li>{datasetStats.doubleRecipientsInThisArchive} people with more than one award in this dataset</li>
    <li>{datasetStats.posthumousAwards.toLocaleString()} awards marked posthumous</li>
    <li>{datasetStats.peopleWithPhoto.toLocaleString()} people with a verified reusable photograph ({datasetStats.photoPercent}%)</li>
    <li>{datasetStats.peopleWithHomeState.toLocaleString()} people with a home state read from the citation entry</li>
    <li>{datasetStats.battles.toLocaleString()} battle and action pages, including {datasetStats.curatedBattles.length} curated hubs</li>
  </ul>
  <p>{datasetStats.officialComparison}</p>

  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <p>
    The roster and citation text come from the U.S. Army Medal of Honor citation index,
    a public-domain government compilation that includes Army, Navy, Marine Corps, Air Force,
    and Coast Guard awards:
    <a href="https://www.army.mil/medalofhonor/recipients.html">army.mil/medalofhonor/recipients.html</a>.
    The pipeline stores a snapshot of each campaign page because some clients are refused by the live site,
    and it can refresh those snapshots from the Internet Archive. Awards published after a snapshot
    are added from Army general orders on the Army Publishing Directorate, which are also government works.
    Each profile links to the source used for that citation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Wikipedia and Wikidata are used only to match a photograph and to offer a background link.
    Wikipedia prose is not copied. Wikimedia material is credited under its own license, including
    CC BY-SA where that is the license on the file. A profile with a Creative Commons photo names
    the license, the credit, and the file page.
  </p>
  <p>
    This project does not copy portraits or prose from the Congressional Medal of Honor Society,
    the National Medal of Honor Museum, Home of Heroes, Military Times, or Honoring American Troops.
    Linking to them from a curated battle page is limited to pages that were checked; the citation
    text itself is taken from government sources.
  </p>

  <h2>Photographs</h2>
  <p>
    A photo is shown only when the person in the file is the recipient and the file may be
    reused. The first pass used Wikidata images on Wikimedia Commons. A later pass added
    portraits from the National Archives Catalog, the Library of Congress Prints and
    Photographs catalog, and additional Commons files. The license has to be public domain,
    CC0, or Creative Commons Attribution, including ShareAlike. Non-commercial and fair-use
    files are left out. Graves, medals, documents, group pictures, and files that do not
    name this recipient are left out. If no such file matches, the page says: “No verified
    public-domain photo is on file yet.” The archive does not use generated or restored likenesses.
  </p>

  <h2>Gaps</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Public totals are usually given as more than 3,500 awards and about 3,526 people after
      the January 3, 2025 ceremony, with further awards in 2026. This archive currently holds
      {datasetStats.people.toLocaleString()} people and {datasetStats.awards.toLocaleString()} awards.
      Added from public Navy, Marine Corps, Army, and DVIDS releases: James Capers Jr.,
      John W. Ripley, Elmer Royce Williams, and Nicholas Dockery. The Unknown Americans of
      World War II and Korea are included from the Army’s special-legislation page.
      Some older general-order scans were still too garbled to publish, and a few citation-index
      entries could not be parsed.
    </li>
    <li>
      The 1917 review rescinded hundreds of earlier awards. Those rescinded awards are not in
      the Army citation index and are not republished here. Restored awards, including Mary Edwards Walker,
      remain when the index includes them.
    </li>
    <li>
      Home state is taken from “accredited to,” “entered service at,” or the birth line when one
      of those names a U.S. state. { (datasetStats.people - datasetStats.peopleWithHomeState).toLocaleString() } people
      have no state in the source line.
    </li>
    <li>
      Posthumous status is marked when the index puts an asterisk by the name or the citation
      says the recipient was killed or gave his or her life. A death the citation attributes to
      someone else is not counted. An asterisk is dropped when the Congressional Medal of Honor
      Society records that the award was not posthumous. Older entries do not always say so.
      The filter is labeled as a marker, not as a complete list of who lived.
    </li>
    <li>
      Place names are the words in the citation entry, lightly cleaned. Some naval citations
      name a ship instead of a town. Curated hubs correct the famous battles; other pages follow the entry.
    </li>
    <li>
      Only the curated hubs have checked articles, footage, books, and films. Other action pages
      say that media has not been curated yet. Footage is linked, never copied onto this site.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Cadences</h2>
  <p>
    Optional background calls are excerpts of U.S. military public-affairs recordings published
    on DVIDS with the public-domain mark and no note of copyrighted music. They never start on
    their own. Credits and units are on the
    <a href="/audio">cadence audio</a> page.
  </p>

  <h2>Build</h2>
  <p>
    The site is a static Astro project. On Render, use build command <code>npm ci && npm run build</code>
    and publish directory <code>dist</code>. Regenerate the dataset with
    <code>python3 scripts/build_dataset.py</code>.
  </p>
  </div>
</Base>
